This directory contains files necessary to generate bootable images for FBOSS using the kiwi ng tool.

`image_builder/templates` contains the template(s) that contain the information needed to build the image.

`centos-09.0` contains the Kiwi-NG template files for building a CentOS-9.0 based FBOSS image. Of particular note
is the `root_files` directory which contains the files that will be copied into the root filesystem of the image.

`onie` contains the template files for building the ONIE installer with the FBOSS image.

KIWI NG Documentation:
    https://osinside.github.io/kiwi/overview.html

Installer networking:
    The 95kiwi-installer-network dracut module is included only when building a
    KIWI dump installer (kiwi-dump). It prevents NetworkManager from adopting
    pre-existing external network configuration instead of activating the
    profiles generated from the boot command line. NetworkManager's existing
    dracut service and nm-online integration are unchanged.

    The policy contains no addresses, DNS servers, interface names, or vendor
    credentials. The provisioning environment supplies ip=, nameserver= and
    related boot arguments. It is not included in a normal NOS initrd and is
    additionally gated by NetworkManager's initrd environment tag.

    Rebuild and publish the complete image bundle to use the updated installer.
    Existing published initrds are not changed. Verify inclusion with:
        lsinitrd -f usr/lib/NetworkManager/conf.d/90-kiwi-installer.conf <installer.initrd>

First-boot reporting:
    The optional 96fboss-firstboot-report dracut module runs after KIWI writes
    and verifies the disk image, before KIWI boots the installed system. When
    the installer command line includes fboss.distro.report.asset_id=<id> and
    fboss.distro.report.url=https://<endpoint>/<path>, it writes those
    arguments into KIWI's /config.bootoptions for its kexec handoff. The hook
    does not mount the target disk or write installed-system configuration.
    No reporting URL is baked into the published image. The module does nothing
    if both arguments are absent.

    The URL must use HTTPS; its path is unrestricted and may include query parameters.
    It must have a non-empty authority and contain no unescaped whitespace or
    control characters. The reporter uses the supplied URL unchanged.

    Argument-transfer failures are logged as warnings and do not prevent the
    installer from booting the already verified image. The hook rejects raw
    whitespace, control characters, quotes and backslashes to prevent changing
    the structure of the next kernel's command line.

    Once the installed Distro reaches network-online.target, the enabled
    fboss-firstboot-report.service reads the reporting arguments from
    /proc/cmdline and validates them before attempting delivery. It does not
    save or read a configuration file. Without reporting boot arguments the
    service does nothing, including on later boots after provisioning.

    The service sends an HTTPS GET to the configured URL
    with name=FBOSS_DISTRO.FIRST_BOOT, assetID=<id>, and
    sender=fboss-distro. A 2xx response creates
    /var/lib/fboss/firstboot-reported. This report
    confirms that the installed system reached userspace with network access.
    KIWI currently kexecs into the installed root filesystem, so the report
    does not independently verify a subsequent firmware/GRUB boot from disk.

    This argument handoff requires KIWI to kexec into the installed OS. A
    firmware reboot does not carry the arguments. Other installation methods
    must supply the reporting arguments on their provisioning boot.

    Redirects are not followed and do not count as successful delivery.
    Configure the final HTTPS endpoint URL; only a 2xx response creates the
    successful-report marker.

    The reporter makes at most three attempts, waiting 15 seconds between
    failures and allowing 20 seconds per attempt. The service has a two-minute
    runtime limit and does not restart automatically after failure. Inspect
    its journal for the error. A manual service restart during the same
    provisioning boot can retry. Retrying after reboot requires provisioning
    to supply the arguments again. Failed delivery does not create the marker.

    Check installer injection with:
        lsinitrd <installer.initrd> | grep 35-transfer-report-args.sh
    On the installed system, inspect:
        systemctl status fboss-firstboot-report.service
        journalctl -u fboss-firstboot-report.service -b
